he polynomial"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3d10ba44223467c583b8666c","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"no_diagnostic_value"},{"id":"la_batch21_root_of_a_polynomial_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does the phrase “over $\\mathbb{F}$” matter when asking for roots?","options":["A candidate root must belong to the field being used","The coefficients are ignored once the field is named","The polynomial degree changes when the field is named","Every field has exactly the same roots for every polynomial"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"781a5f5f597a458a504d3c96","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"la_batch21_root_of_a_polynomial_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does a root reveal about divisibility by a linear factor, when polynomial division is available?","options":["It means the polynomial is constant","It suggests that $x-\\lambda$ divides the polynomial","It prevents polynomial long division","It means $\\lambda$ is the leading coefficient"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0a8309b48f9c62b5f9bf4723","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"la_batch21_root_of_a_polynomial_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For $p(x)=x^3-8$, which number is a root?","options":["$4$","$-2$","$2$","$8$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"19714adde794f8c0f37afb03","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"la_batch21_root_of_a_polynomial_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For $p(x)=x^2-4x+4$, which statement about roots is correct?","options":["There are no real roots","$2$ is a root","Both $0$ and $4$ are roots","$-2$ is a root"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2881943aa354c39418233f40","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"la_batch21_root_of_a_polynomial_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $p(5)=7$, what can be concluded about $5$?","options":["$5$ is a root of $p$","$7$ is the only root of $p$","$5$ is not a root of $p$","The polynomial has no roots at all"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"237a0dfa3b32dfcf35856486","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"la_batch21_root_of_a_polynomial_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For a nonzero polynomial, roots are inputs giving zero. Why is the zero polynomial exceptional?","options":["The zero polynomial has exactly one root, namely the scalar $0$","Every scalar makes the zero polynomial evaluate to zero","The zero polynomial is not a polynomial","The zero polynomial has no input values"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a31ed483ea7fdab2f66cadf7","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"la_batch21_root_of_a_polynomial_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Over $\\mathbb{R}$, how many roots does $p(x)=x^2+1$ have?","options":["$1$","$2$","Infinitely many","$0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ed1b203310e3d3d85541fb93","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"la_batch21_root_of_a_polynomial_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","q